<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Tomato</h1></b><br>

    Tomatoes are one of the most widely grown crops in the UK, both in <strong>greenhouses</strong> and <strong>outdoor gardens</strong>. 
    While they can be cultivated across the country, the best results are typically seen in <strong>southern England</strong>, where summers 
    are warmer and longer. In <strong>northern regions and Scotland</strong>, tomatoes can still be grown successfully, but they often require 
    <strong>greenhouse protection</strong> or <strong>polytunnels</strong> to ensure proper ripening and disease prevention.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Prefers warm, sunny conditions with minimal temperature fluctuations.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, nutrient-rich soil with a pH between 6.0 and 6.8.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Requires at least 6–8 hours of direct sunlight daily.<br>
    - <strong>Watering:</strong> Needs consistent watering, keeping soil moist but not waterlogged. Avoid watering the leaves.<br>
    - <strong>Support:</strong> Indeterminate (vining) varieties need stakes, cages, or trellises for support.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Tomato Diseases in the UK</h2></b><br>

    - <strong>Early Blight</strong><br>
      <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Dark brown spots with concentric rings on lower leaves, leading to yellowing and defoliation.<br>
      <strong>Prevention & Treatment:</strong><br>
      - Rotate crops yearly to prevent soil contamination.<br>
      - Avoid overhead watering and ensure good airflow around plants.<br>
      - Remove infected leaves and apply copper-based fungicides if needed.<br><br>

    - <strong>Late Blight</strong><br>
      <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Large, dark, greasy-looking spots on leaves and fruit, spreading rapidly in wet conditions.<br>
      <strong>Prevention & Treatment:</strong><br>
      - Grow resistant varieties like ‘Crimson Crush’.<br>
      - Space plants properly to improve airflow and reduce moisture retention.<br>
      - Remove and destroy infected plants immediately to prevent spread.<br><br>

    - <strong>Septoria Leaf Spot</strong><br>
      <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Small, circular brown spots with dark edges on leaves, leading to early leaf drop.<br>
      <strong>Prevention & Treatment:</strong><br>
      - Keep leaves dry by watering at the base of the plant.<br>
      - Prune lower leaves to reduce humidity around the plant.<br>
      - Apply fungicides if the disease is severe.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Tomatoes in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Choose the right variety:</strong> ‘Gardener’s Delight’, ‘Shirley’, and ‘Alicante’ are reliable choices for the UK.<br>
    - <strong>Feed regularly:</strong> Use a high-potassium fertilizer (like tomato feed) once flowering begins.<br>
    - <strong>Pinch out side shoots:</strong> Helps concentrate energy on fruit production for indeterminate varieties.<br>
    - <strong>Protect from cold:</strong> In cooler areas, start seeds indoors and transplant after the last frost.<br>
    - <strong>Harvesting:</strong> Pick tomatoes when they are fully colored but still firm for the best flavor.<br><br>

    Tomatoes are a rewarding crop to grow in the UK, especially with proper disease prevention and care.
    </i></p>
